[Music]  hi everyone my name is Sarah random  facts about me I don't have a large  intestine yeah that's because I have  Crohn's disease also an IBD and advocate  who runs a website and a YouTube channel  my name is Dan sharp  i'ma Crohn's patient and in IBD advocate  that's what this channel is about it's  about my life with Crohn's disease if  you don't know what that is then all the  more reason to subscribe and figure out  how I [ __ ] I'm trying to show that good  information is accessible to patients  you don't have to be a scientist or  doctor to go and figure out this stuff  [Music]  now with the Internet patients can have  a lot more information that's the way  the world is going  I've used a phrase previously that I  borrowed from somebody that it takes a  village to take care of my B deviation  and to me that village includes other  patients Crohn's is a type of IBD IV d  stands for inflammatory bowel disease  really it's a group of disorders or  conditions  wherein the immune system has an  overactive response to something in the  digestive tract and ends up causing  inflammation and damage these aren't  like any other chronic medical condition  sometimes people call these the  invisible diseases because they'll say  something like well you look fine you  might as well be okay imagine then if  you have a bowel condition that provides  you with a lack of control or a feeling  like you don't know what's going to  happen next you are unable to plan for  the future and you may not actually have  people around you who understand it  enough to help you or to support you the  way you need I started advocacy in a  weird way it wasn't it wasn't till like  three years after my first two surgeries  but I ended up in the hospital again  so to fill my time and to feel like  something was happening in my day I just  opened my computer one day and turned on  my webcam and started talking to the  camera and started sharing my story and  uploading that to YouTube and then it  turned into what it is today I think  that it's important that the right story  about IBD gets  out there to the general public that's  why I share what I do I want to just  break down the stigma that is all we are  is people that are running to the  bathroom all the time because that's  definitely not my life and it's not a  lot of our lives the more that I  uploaded videos and I saw the response  and patients responded to it so well I  was like okay I'm doing a good thing  like this is okay and it inspired me to  talk my advocacy something I'm really  passionate about is helping patients be  empowered with information I think more  that a patient knows about their  condition the better decisions they can  make about the health the better they  can advocate in themselves in a  healthcare setting like they know what  questions to ask things like that  the traditional model of patience  learning about their disease came from a  much more paternalistic relationship  with their doctors the doctor would tell  them what they had and what they needed  to do and the patient either would do it  or they wouldn't but more often than not  they would do it we evolved into the  appreciation for shared decision-making  and shared decision-making was augmented  by the growth of the internet now at the  same time we also learned a lot more  about how patients make decisions and  the value of other educational fora  including YouTube videos now whenever  you open up channels of information  there's also the challenge of facing  misinformation rather than be threatened  by that I think physicians and nurses  need to be open to that dialogue and  understand what's out there this is  groundbreaking stuff okay you're gone I  know Dan and Sarah very well he's pretty  much everywhere there we go if we go to  a conference or something he's most  likely presenting at least ones there I  actually look up to them and I respect  what they do greatly in fact I think  that they really believe and I  completely support their role as saying  you know what we're not part of the  medical establishment but we're informed  and we want you to know that what you're  saying isn't right and what has been  necessary in order for them to do that  so effectively is for them to actually  attend medical conferences to not be  afraid of learning the science and  challenging the science and you know I  might not be a doctor but I'm a smart  person I'm capable of learning a lot so  I put my time and energy into  researching things making sure I get the  right information and then I always  check it before I publish it I have a  lot of doctors on the internet that have  been so kind to me and let me weave  messaged them late at night on Twitter  and be like hey I have a question and  I'm not afraid to give feedback if I  think they're misdirecting people  because it's going to happen either way  you might as well be doing it and  working together then having any kind of  fear of what that means I always kind of  joke around about like well if a doctor  retweets it then that means I did a good  job  they were saying that this was good  information that I'm like yeah the  information I was taught by my mentor  that all the research we do should start  and end with the patient who suffers and  that may sound completely obvious but  the reality is that in research a lot of  times it's that there are certain things  being studied because patients in the  IVD community are speaking up and so for  years what was the number-one topic on  the internet that wasn't able to be  answered sufficiently in the IVD  research arena I would say that it was  diet-related there's a study going on  right now through the CCFA about  studying two different kinds of diets  and what effect they might have on  inflammatory bowel disease that's an  area that a lot of patients feel are  important but that I really think was a  crowd source I think that anyone who's  not paying attention to what's going on  out there is turning a blind eye on the  most important information that they  could have about what research needs to  be done  good luck  you

